<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.12.2: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00585</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.12.2: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</md:title>
    <md:uuid>99539b32-fe44-47b0-ad49-c171b7466d4e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">For questions 1 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <quote id="quote-00001">The homeowner is worried about the work needed to maintain a grass lawn and flower beds, so she is now looking at some low-maintenance materials.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00002">She is considering a combination of the materials shown to cover the yard. Her budget is still $3,000.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00003">Here is a graph representing some constraints in this situation.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003"> The graph shows a line going through \((500,100)\). In this situation, what does the point \((500,100)\) mean?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004"/>
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">The cost of installing 500 square feet of gravel and 100 square feet of artificial turf is $3000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"> Write an equation that the line represents.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008"/>
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010"> \(3x + 15y = 3000\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011"> What do the solutions to the equation mean?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012"/>
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">The solutions are all combinations of square feet of gravel and artificial turf that would cost $3000 to install.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015"> The point \((600,200)\) is located to the right and above the line. Does that combination of turf and gravel meet the homeowner's constraints? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016"/>
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">No. Answers may vary, but here is a sample. If she installs 600 square feet of gravel and 200 square feet of turf, the cost is \(3(600)+15(200)\), which is \(1800 + 3,000\) or \($4800\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019"> Choose another point in the same region (to the right and above the line). Check if the combination meets the homeowner's constraints.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00020"/>
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Answers may vary, but here is a sample. \((900,50)\). This combination would cost more than $3000 to install.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00023"> The point \((200,100)\) is located to the left and below the line. Does that combination of turf and gravel meet the homeowner's constraints? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00024"/>
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026">Yes. Answers may vary, but here is a sample: \(3(200) + 15(100) = 2100\). This amount is less than $3000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00027"> Choose another point in the same region (to the left and below the line). Check if the combination meets the homeowner's constraints.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00028"/>
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Answers may vary, but here is a sample: \((600, 25)\). This combination would cost under $3000 to install.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00031"> Write an inequality that represents the constraints in this situation. Explain what the solutions mean and show the solution region on the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00034">Answers may vary, but here is a sample:</para>
            <para id="para-00035">\(3x + 15y\leq3000\). The solutions represent all possible combinations of square feet of gravel and turf that would cost no more than $3000 to install.</para>
            <figure id="fig-00001">
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality. Artificial turf, square feet. Gravel, square feet.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_14_03_01.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</title>
      <para id="para-00036">Watch the following video to learn more about representing constraints using inequalities.
</para>
